# THE STEPPER #

# A proof is a chain of goals. It starts at the statement, and each #
# step replaces the goal with one that is enough to prove it, until #
# the goal is true. The stepper draws the chain, one row per goal, #
# and underlines the part of each goal that the next step acts on. #

# The stepper and the proof text are two views of one proof: editing #
# either changes the other. You can write a proof in the stepper and #
# only read the text it writes. #

# An unfinished proof ends in a write box. Click the box in the #
# stepper for try_it below, then: #
#   Tab     takes the next evaluation step, shown in grey #
#   type    the goal you want next, such as 21 == 21, and press #
#           Enter: a search finds the steps that get there #
#   select  part of the goal, such as 3 + 4, to work on just that #
#           part #
# The tabs above the box offer more: Step marks each part of the #
# goal that can be evaluated, and Strategy writes the structured #
# steps of the later slides. #

theorem try_it = (1 + 2) * (3 + 4) == 21 proof ¿ in
¿
